#---
# purpose: "a legal recursive @type declaration that the compiler cannot yet resolve"
# expect: diagnostic
# diagnostic.code: resolve/type-alias
#---
#
# `t` is a legal recursive declaration: it is `:start` or a pair whose second
# field is another `t`. Resolving it needs a recursive denotation — a type whose
# definition names itself. The compiler has none, so `DeriveTypeDef(t)` waits on
# `TypeDefined(t)`, which is its own output: a job waiting on the very fact it
# would produce, so nothing ever runs it.
#
# `World::unresolved_issue` recognizes a self-referential `TypeDefined` wait and
# reports it at the `@type` declaration's own span, so every door names the same
# problem: `t` could not be resolved because its definition waits on itself.
def main() do
  dbg(build(3))
end

@type t :: :start | {integer, t}

@spec build(integer) :: t
def build(0), do: :start
def build(n), do: {n, build(n - 1)}
